OK, so the story behind this weirdly shaped Blue Oak tree is as follows: 1) first there was a fertile acorn;  2) many years later, the Oak tree was partially blown over towards the West, but some of the lateral branches then gave secondary support to the tilted tree;  3) then along came some humans that built the house and an above-ground pool;  4) the second owners built extensive decking around the back of the house and the above-ground pool, and in the process the deck construction crew foolishly sawed off the secondary support branches (last photo);  5) the grandkids grew up and the pool liner got solarized in the HOT Sun and was torn;  6) Ken and Karla bought the house, demolished the pool (got $5.40 for recycled bulk metal), and removed most of the pool decking; and 7) using a wooden joist beam from beneath a portion of the removed decking, we installed a tree support post to slow the Blue Oak tree's "falling down" process ......... 

Many creatures have used this leaning "Moose Tree" as their home over the years:  
1) a Raccoon in 2005 (www.inaturalist.org/observations/91473804); 
2) another Raccoon in 2013 (www.inaturalist.org/observations/89029785); 
3) an Opossum in 2015 (www.inaturalist.org/observations/89028883);  
4) numerous birds, including Starlings in 2008 (www.inaturalist.org/observations/117782494) and 
White-breasted Nuthatches in 2009 (www.inaturalist.org/observations/117783711); and 
5) numerous Fox Squirrels (www.inaturalist.org/observations/94772165 in 2008) and (www.inaturalist.org/observations/161139244 in 2023).  

The Big Question nowadays is "Which way will the Oak tree begin to lean towards", and I hope that we never have to cut it down ......... the "Moose Tree" has so much character and it has given us so much enjoyment .......... and tons and tons of Acorns .......... 
P.S.  I think that it looks more like a Reindeer rather than a Moose, but who knows ........

Looking for hair tufts along mid-rib underside: *Q*. *velutina* has this, not *Q*. *rubra*, & I learned to show it;
For the Rust Fungi [looks like "pollen", photos 4-8], *cf*. inaturalist.org/observations/161343565;
Subfamily Calaphidinae, Aphids Family Aphididae [9-13], *cf*. inaturalist.org/observations/162528955;
For the Tribe Empoascini leafhopper [19th photo], *cf*. inaturalist.org/observations/162528324;
For the same Black Oak (*Quercus velutina*) the day before, *cf*. inaturalist.org/observations/161180906

